# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Docker build context exclusions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Before a build starts, Docker sends the *build context* — everything in this
# directory — to the daemon. Whatever is not excluded here is transferred, is
# walked on every build, and is available to any `COPY . .`.
#
# Two reasons this file matters. Speed: `.venv/` alone is ~190 MB of material
# the image never uses, since dependencies are installed inside the image.
# Safety: a local `.env` swept into the context can be copied into a
# distributable image, embedding real credentials in it.
#
# NOT interchangeable with .gitignore. This file must exclude `.git` itself,
# which .gitignore by definition never does. The syntax also differs: patterns
# are anchored to the context root, so nested matches need an explicit `**/`
# prefix — `__pycache__` matches only the top level, `**/__pycache__` matches
# every level.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Version control and CI
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.git` is the single largest avoidable item in most repositories and grows
# with every commit. Nothing in the image reads project history.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment and secrets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The security-relevant section. A real `.env` must never enter the context:
# once it is there, one careless `COPY . .` bakes live credentials into an
# image that may be pushed to a registry. Configuration reaches the container
# at run time, through the environment — never at build time.
# `.env.example` is the committed template and carries no real values, so it is
# re-included. The negation must follow the patterns it overrides.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Virtual environments
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The bulk of the context. A host virtualenv is also built for the host's
# platform and interpreter, so copying one in would be actively wrong: the
# image builds its own with `uv sync`.
.venv
venv
ENV
env
env.bak
venv.bak

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python caches and build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Regenerated inside the image (the builder sets UV_COMPILE_BYTECODE=1).
# Host-built artifacts can carry the wrong architecture.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*$py.class
**/*.so
**/*.egg-info
**/*.egg
build
dist
sdist
wheels
develop-eggs
eggs
.eggs
MANIFEST

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test, coverage and type-checking caches
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Machine-local state. Test tooling is excluded from the runtime image anyway
# (`uv sync --no-dev`), so its caches have nothing to say here.
.pytest_cache
.tox
.nox
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov
.cache
.mypy_cache
.dmypy.json
dmypy.json
.pyre
.pytype
.ruff_cache

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documentation and project meta
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prose is for people reading the repository, not for the running service.
docs
CONTRIBUTING.md
CHANGELOG.md
# README.md and LICENSE are the exceptions: pyproject.toml declares
# `readme = "README.md"` and `license-files = ["LICENSE"]`, so the build
# backend needs both present the moment the project itself is installed
# (M3-01, when `uv sync` drops `--no-install-project`). Excluding them fails
# the build with a confusing "file not found" from the backend.
!README.md
!LICENSE

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Docker's own files
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Dockerfile is read out of band by the daemon, not from the context, so
# excluding it is safe and keeps it from being copied into the image.
Dockerfile*
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml
docker-compose*.yaml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local working documents
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mirrors .gitignore: planning notes and assistant context, kept on disk but
# neither committed nor shipped.
**/*.local.*
CLAUDE.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Django runtime data
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated or user-supplied at run time, and environment-specific. Static
# files are collected during the build or at startup, never copied from a
# developer's machine.
db.sqlite3
db.sqlite3-journal
staticfiles
mediafiles
media
**/*.log
**/local_settings.py

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors, IDEs and operating systems
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode
.idea
**/*.iml
**/*.swp
**/*.swo
**/*~
.spyderproject
.spyproject
.ropeproject
**/.DS_Store
**/._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
**/Thumbs.db
**/ehthumbs.db
**/desktop.ini
